      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sobieski was named in honor of King John III Sobieski, a seventeenth-century Polish monarch known for his victory at the Battle of Vienna.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sobieski%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Polish immigrants settled in Sobieski during the late nineteenth century, establishing farms and businesses amid the area's dense forests and rivers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sobieski_wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Logging barons originally controlled the land in Sobieski, exploiting the northern hardwood forests of Oconto County for timber harvesting.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sobieski%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Rail service from the early nineteen hundreds supported local industries like a cheese factory, a blacksmith shop, and a meat market.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sobieski_wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Cultural life in Sobieski centers on Polish Catholic traditions, highlighted by the St. John Cantius Catholic Church.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sobieski_wisconsin"
    },
    {
